<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Peer Review Policy - AJSMR</title><style>body{font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;margin:0;background:#f5f6f8;color:#222;line-height:1.65}.container{max-width:1050px;margin:35px auto;padding:0 20px}.card{background:#fff;border-radius:8px;padding:32px;box-shadow:0 2px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.08)}h1{margin-top:0;font-size:30px}h2{font-size:21px;margin-top:28px}h3{font-size:18px;margin-top:22px}.note{background:#f0f4f8;padding:14px 16px;border-left:4px solid #555;margin:20px 0}.small{font-size:13px;color:#666}</style></head><body><div class="container"><div class="card"><h1>Peer Review Policy</h1>
<div class="note">AJSMR uses external peer review for primary research articles. The journal's website and editorial system should accurately reflect the review process used for every article category.</div>
<h2>1. Purpose of Peer Review</h2>
<p>Peer review helps editors assess the scientific quality, originality, methodological soundness, clarity, ethical compliance and relevance of submitted manuscripts.</p>
<h2>2. Review Model</h2>
<p>AJSMR describes its review model as <strong>double-blind peer review</strong>, in which the identities of authors and reviewers are not routinely disclosed to each other during the review process. The journal should apply this model consistently where the manuscript and editorial system permit it.</p>
<h2>3. Number of Reviewers</h2>
<p>For primary research articles, AJSMR should obtain reports from <strong>at least two independent external reviewers</strong> before a final publication decision, consistent with the journal's stated quality-control process. If an exception is necessary, the editor should document the reason and ensure adequate independent assessment.</p>
<h2>4. Reviewer Selection</h2>
<p>Reviewers are selected for relevant subject expertise and should be independent of the authors and their institutions. Editors should avoid reviewers with personal, professional, financial or other conflicts that could compromise impartiality.</p>
<h2>5. Reviewer Responsibilities</h2>
<ul><li>Assess the manuscript objectively and provide constructive, evidence-based comments.</li><li>Maintain confidentiality of the manuscript and review process.</li><li>Identify important relevant published work where appropriate.</li><li>Alert the editor to suspected plagiarism, duplicate publication, data concerns or other research-integrity issues.</li><li>Declare conflicts of interest before accepting or completing a review.</li><li>Complete reviews within the agreed timeframe or notify the editor if an extension is required.</li></ul>
<h2>6. Reviewer Confidentiality</h2>
<p>Reviewers must not share manuscripts, figures, data or confidential review information with unauthorized persons. Manuscripts should not be uploaded to external generative AI systems where this could compromise confidentiality or intellectual property.</p>
<h2>7. Editorial Assessment</h2>
<p>Editors may assess submissions before external review for scope, completeness, ethical concerns, originality, methodological suitability and other basic requirements. Such editorial screening does not replace external peer review for primary research articles.</p>
<h2>8. Editorial Decisions</h2>
<p>After considering reviewer reports and the editor's own assessment, the editor may decide to accept, request revision, reject, or seek additional review. Reviewer recommendations are advisory; the final decision remains an editorial responsibility.</p>
<h2>9. Revision</h2>
<p>When revision is invited, authors should respond to reviewer comments clearly and indicate changes made in the manuscript. Revised manuscripts may be returned to the original reviewers or evaluated by another reviewer where appropriate.</p>
<h2>10. Reviewer Disagreement</h2>
<p>If reviewer recommendations substantially conflict, the editor may obtain an additional independent review or make a decision based on the available evidence and editorial assessment.</p>
<h2>11. Appeals</h2>
<p>Authors may appeal an editorial decision through the journal's Complaints & Appeals process. An appeal should identify the decision concerned and provide a reasoned explanation based on the manuscript and review process.</p>
<h2>12. Special Issues and Guest Editors</h2>
<p>Special-issue manuscripts should receive the same level of editorial oversight and external peer review as regular manuscripts. The Editor-in-Chief remains responsible for the integrity of the journal's publication process, including special issues. Guest editors should not make decisions on manuscripts where they have a conflict.</p>
<h2>13. Editor or Board Member Submissions</h2>
<p>When an editor, editorial-board member or other person with an editorial role submits a manuscript, the manuscript should be handled independently by an editor without a conflict of interest. The submitting editor should not participate in the decision on the manuscript.</p>
<h2>14. Peer Review Records</h2>
<p>The editorial system should retain appropriate records of reviewer invitations, reviewer identities, reports, decisions and communications, subject to applicable privacy and confidentiality requirements.</p>
<h2>15. Transparency</h2>
<p>The peer-review process described on this page must match the journal's actual practice. Any changes to the review model should be reflected promptly on the website and in the editorial management system.</p><p class="small">AJSMR local testing version. Final publication should be checked against actual journal procedures before deployment to the live website.</p></div></div></body></html>